  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1...Showers and scattered thunderstorms expected again \ntoday, then drier and less humid Sunday. \n\nA few showers will be possible there the early overnight hours, \nmainly for our counties east of Lake Ontario associated with the \ndeparting shortwave. Otherwise, a soupy airmass (humid, dwpts 60s to \nnear 70F) remain over the Lower Great Lakes and temps in the 60s to \naround 70F. The next feature of interest is a mid-level convectively \naugment shortwave tracking through the base of the broad trough. \nLatest CAMs show the convection over Ohio expanding east and \nnortheast and arriving between 09Z-11Z across far Western New York, \nthen work east across the region. Additional chances for showers and \nstorms will then be possible this afternoon, especially along lake \nbreeze boundaries as MLCAPE values ramp up to +2500 j/kg. Given the \nsoupy airmass (PW 1.5\"-1.7\") heavy rainfall will 'likely' be the \nmain threat which could cause some localized issues. The threat for \nsevere weather still appears low at this time. As was noted, bulk \nshear values remain on the low side (25 knots). That said, a few \nstorms still could produce strong gusty winds.  \n\nAs we work though the evening hours the weak cold front will arrive \nbringing the potential for some more storms as it crosses the \nregion. After that...we will see a slow but gradual drying trend \novernight. A weak surface ridge crosses the region Sunday which will \nprovide mainly dry weather and overall comfortable humidity level. \n\nKey Message 2...Unsettled weather returns for the Lower Great Lakes \nto start the new work week.\n\nA zonal flow early next week will begin to shift southward, with a \nmore noticeable dip late in the week as a deepening upper level low \ndrops southward from Canada. \n\nA moisture rich airmass will remain through the beginning of next \nweek, one that will support not only afternoon convection, but the \npossibility of overnight convection, though exact timing of subtle \nshortwave troughs is with still great uncertainty at this point in \ntime.",
